{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nSupporters of the law argue that it allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources, while opponents claim it may create a confusing and inequitable system. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the criminal justice system.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, people across the country will be watching to see how it plays out, and at what age individuals are most affected. It's a complex issue, and one that may leave some feeling like they're walking on thin ice, not knowing whether to eat away their worries or go shopping to distract themselves - all while biting their tongue. \nP.P.S As the debate continues, it's clear that the issue is a sensitive one, with many factors at play, and the outcome will be closely watched by those across the country.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.059865078597795e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0016543286619707942}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -1.011